From f44accde67e0ded2323f21b81091e5e17d0db80b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: kernel-wasm <kernel-wasm@localhost>
Date: Mon, 16 Nov 2026 16:07:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] wasm: SMP

A second build of the same arch (kw_smp.config: CONFIG_SMP, NR_CPUS
16), which always links with a shared memory. Each CPU is an instance
of the kernel module in its own host thread on that memory; current,
__stack_pointer, the interrupt flag (kw_irq_flags) and the address of
the CPU's word of pending interrupts (kw_irq_word) are wasm globals of
the instance. The host offers wasm_cpu_count() CPUs, and __cpu_up()
asks it to start one with wasm_cpu_start(): the new instance enters at
kw_secondary_entry with its idle task. The word that guards the data
segments of a shared memory (__wasm_init_memory_flag, past _end) is
memblock-reserved at the address the host reads from the module
(wasm_init_flag()), so later instances skip the initialisation.

A task's stack lives in the thread that started it, so the task is
held on that CPU (ARCH_TASKS_STAY_ON_CPU) from its first run, except
where nothing on the stack is needed again: a user task at the
reschedule on its way to user mode (its state is all in pt_regs), a
kthread at a restart point. Switched in on another CPU it starts there
on a fresh stack (a user task runs the exit to user mode again, a
kthread its restart function), and the CPU that holds the old stack
tells its host to drop it (wasm_stack_drop()) at its next switch or
idle; switched in on its own CPU it resumes its stack. An affinity
change that excludes a held user task's CPU sets TIF_KW_MOVE, so the
task moves on its way to user mode (at once from user mode: the
change kicks it). So does a better CPU the scheduler names for a held
user task (arch_stay_move_to(): exec, a wakeup on its busy CPU), which
the task takes on its way to user mode only if that CPU is idle then
and its own CPU has other work. /proc/kwmove counts the moves.
copy_thread() drops the hold a fork copied.

IPIs are bits in the target's word (CPU 0's is wasm_host_irqs, where
the host's device bits arrive), set atomically and followed by
memory.atomic.notify. A CPU takes them at user slice ends, in idle
(secondaries block in memory.atomic.wait32 with the timer's timeout;
CPU 0 in the host's poll_oneoff), and, on SMP only, when interrupts
are enabled and in cpu_relax(), so a CPU spinning on a lock answers
the holder's TLB shootdown. A CPU spinning with interrupts off still
takes the stop IPI, which a panic sends and which a CPU's host raises
on CPU 0 when the session ends there. The timer is a per-CPU
clockevent on a percpu-devid IRQ 0.

TLB flushes reach the other CPUs in mm_cpumask (kept by switch_mm())
by IPI and wait for them, except a CPU in lazy TLB mode (idle or a
kthread, with the mm still loaded), which is marked stale and flushes
when it switches back to that mm; a PTE that only gained access is
flushed on this CPU alone, since kwemu caches only translations that
allowed the access. riscv_flush_icache flushes every CPU. The software
A/D update is a cmpxchg, so every read-modify-write of a present PTE
is atomic (but for the clear in the teardown of a whole mm, which no
CPU uses any more), and the uaccess walk and copy have no poll point
between them, so pages freed after a shootdown are never copied
into. kwemu gets its own state per CPU (8 MiB from memblock
for the secondaries), the WASI clock and write buffers are per CPU,
and every CPU yields to its own host every 20 ms of user code: CPU 0's
serves the devices, the others' event loops run the engine's
housekeeping (collecting the modules kwemu's JIT freed on that CPU).
Parking (KW_PARK) is off.

kw_emu.h follows kwemu's ABI 7 (several harts on one memory).
